Requesting a CodeAction for the word `Vec` in the example above results in 185 assists in the "Generate delegate methods" action group.  After the user selects one from the list, the LSP client requests to resolve the selected CodeAction.  However, rust-analyzer generates edits for the all the 185 assists, which in my case takes more than 4 seconds. 

I work on a feature outline [here](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-analyzer/issues/19090#issuecomment-2644742013), so in reality rust-analyzer might answer the resolve request faster, it is sub-optimal nevertheless. 

The inefficiency caused by the fact that the `AssistId`s of all the 185 assists are the same, therefore all of them are resolved during a resolve request.

I am able to bring the answer time down to around 200 milliseconds by generating a different index for each "generate delegate" assists.  This requires extending `struct AssisId` like this:
```patch
diff --git a/crates/ide-db/src/assists.rs b/crates/ide-db/src/assists.rs
index 1c40685ebb..14f3280bb5 100644
--- a/crates/ide-db/src/assists.rs
+++ b/crates/ide-db/src/assists.rs
@@ -104,8 +104,8 @@ impl FromStr for AssistKind {

 /// Unique identifier of the assist, should not be shown to the user
 /// directly.
-#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
-pub struct AssistId(pub &'static str, pub AssistKind);
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct AssistId(pub &'static str, pub AssistKind, pub Option<usize>);

 /// A way to control how many assist to resolve during the assist resolution.
 /// When an assist is resolved, its edits are calculated that might be costly to always do by default.
```
