## Description

This is a bug of `lib/line_index` version `0.1.0`. It seems that rust-analyzer is using version 0.1.0-pre.1.

The comment here explains that `start` and `end` are offsets inside a single line:

https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-analyzer/blob/2ee17bc5f256e379c4df954e984b9bd0d79f1cbd/lib/line-index/src/lib.rs#L55-L60

However, during text analysis, the offsets stored in `WideChar` are byte offsets of the entire text.

https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-analyzer/blob/2ee17bc5f256e379c4df954e984b9bd0d79f1cbd/lib/line-index/src/lib.rs#L366-L372

Finally, when calculating `LineCol`, the `start` and `end` in `WideChar` are treated as offsets inside the line again.

https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-analyzer/blob/2ee17bc5f256e379c4df954e984b9bd0d79f1cbd/lib/line-index/src/lib.rs#L118-L132

## Example

The code below will panic.

```rust
fn main() {
    let text = "\n\n\n\n\n宽3456";
    assert_eq!(&text[5..8], "宽");
    assert_eq!(&text[11..12], "6");
    let line_index = LineIndex::new(text);
    let before_6 = TextSize::from(11);
    assert!(line_index.try_line_col(before_6).is_some()); // This will panic!
}
```

The line offset of the character `6` is `6..7`.
The global offset of the character `宽` is `5..8`, and it is mistakenly used as line offset.

