r-a may fail to type coercion for variables in a closure.
In the following example, type coercion should occurs for the former value of the if statement, but r-a does not and reports type mismatch for the latter value.

```rust
fn foo() {
    let bar = || {
        if true {
            &"str"
        } else {
            "str"
            //^ expected &&str, found &str
        }
    };
}
```

However, in the example below, r-a won't report type mismatch.
So I suspect that r-a is only applying type coercion to the latter variable of the closure which return type is omitted.

```rust
fn foo() {
    let bar = || {
        if true {
            "str"
        } else {
            &"str"
        }
    };

    let bar = || -> &str {
        if true {
            &"str"
        } else {
            "str"
        }
    };

    let bar = || {
        let x = if true { // -> &str
            &"str"
        } else {
            "str"
        };

        x
    };

}
```

